{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013Pqp1roVxfp7Nmp3jZsR2A","type":"tool_result","content":"\tdefaultIntakeMode      = IntakeLabel\n\n\tdefaultIntervalScanners  = Duration(4 * time.Hour)\n\tdefaultIntervalTriage    = Duration(15 * time.Minute)\n\tdefaultIntervalWorkers   = Duration(5 * time.Minute)\n\tdefaultIntervalReviewers = Duration(5 * time.Minute)\n\tdefaultIntervalMerger    = Duration(5 * time.Minute)\n\tdefaultIntervalPRWatch   = Duration(5 * time.Minute)\n\n\tdefaultPRWatchPendingTimeout = Duration(2 * time.Hour)\n\tmaxPRWatchPendingTimeout     = Duration(7 * 24 * time.Hour)\n\t\tBudgetUSDPerDay: defaultBudgetUSDPerDay,\n\t\tMaxBudgetUSD:    defaultMaxBudgetUSD,\n\t\tGlobalMaxOpen:   defaultGlobalMaxOpen,\n\t\tIntervals: Intervals{\n\t\t\tScanners:  defaultIntervalScanners,\n\t\t\tTriage:    defaultIntervalTriage,\n\t\t\tWorkers:   defaultIntervalWorkers,\n\t\t\tReviewers: defaultIntervalReviewers,\n\t\t\tMerger:    defaultIntervalMerger,\n\t\t\tPRWatch:   defaultIntervalPRWatch,\n\t\t},\n\t\tPRWatch: PRWatch{PendingTimeout: defaultPRWatchPendingTimeout},\n\t\tIntake:  Intake{Mode: defaultIntakeMode},\n\tfor _, iv := range []struct {\n\t\tkey string\n\t\td   Duration\n\t}{\n\t\t{\"intervals.scanners\", res.Intervals.Scanners},\n\t\t{\"intervals.triage\", res.Intervals.Triage},\n\t\t{\"intervals.workers\", res.Intervals.Workers},\n\t\t{\"intervals.reviewers\", res.Intervals.Reviewers},\n\t\t{\"intervals.merger\", res.Intervals.Merger},\n\t\t{\"intervals.prwatch\", res.Intervals.PRWatch},\n\t} {\n\t\tif iv.d < minLoopInterval {\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, &Error{File: cfgPath, Key: iv.key, Msg: fmt.Sprintf(\"must be at least 1m (the Reaper alone runs every minute), got %s\", iv.d)})\n\t\t}\n\t}\nfunc Keys() []string {\n\treturn []string{\n\t\t\"max_pods\", \"executor\", \"agent\", \"require_approval\",\n\t\t\"budget_usd_per_day\", \"tokens_per_day\", \"max_budget_usd\", \"global_max_open\", \"paused\",\n\t\t\"intervals.scanners\", \"intervals.triage\", \"intervals.workers\",\n\t\t\"intervals.reviewers\", \"intervals.merger\", \"intervals.prwatch\",\n\t\t\"pr_watch.pending_timeout\", \"roles_repo\",\n\t\t\"intake.mode\", \"intake.label\",\n\t\t\"merge.protected_paths\",\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc (ri *rawIntervals) parse(n *yaml.Node, file, prefix string) Errors {\n\tvar errs Errors\n\tif n.Tag == \"!!null\" {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tif n.Kind != yaml.MappingNode {\n\t\treturn Errors{{File: file, Key: prefix, Msg: \"must be a mapping of loop intervals\"}}\n\t}\n\tset := func(name string, dst **Duration) {\n\t\tvalue := mappingValue(n, name)\n\t\tif value == nil {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tvar d Duration\n\t\tbefore := len(errs)\n\t\terrs = append(errs, decodeValue(value, file, prefix+\".\"+name, &d)...)\n\t\tif len(errs) == before {\n\t\t\t*dst = &d\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tset(\"scanners\", &ri.scanners)\n\tset(\"triage\", &ri.triage)\n\tset(\"workers\", &ri.workers)\n\tset(\"reviewers\", &ri.reviewers)\n\tset(\"merger\", &ri.merger)\n\tset(\"prwatch\", &ri.prwatch)\n\tfor i := 0; i < len(n.Content); i += 2 {\n\t\tname := n.Content[i].Value\n\t\tswitch name {\n\t\tcase \"scanners\", \"triage\", \"workers\", \"reviewers\", \"merger\", \"prwatch\":\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, &Error{File: file, Key: prefix + \".\" + name, Msg: \"unknown key (want one of scanners, triage, workers, reviewers, merger, prwatch)\"})\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn errs\n}\n\nfunc (raw *rawConfig) parseMerge(n *yaml.Node, file string) Errors {\ntype intervalsYAML struct {\n\tScanners  Duration `yaml:\"scanners\"`\n\tTriage    Duration `yaml:\"triage\"`\n\tWorkers   Duration `yaml:\"workers\"`\n\tReviewers Duration `yaml:\"reviewers\"`\n\tMerger    Duration `yaml:\"merger\"`\n\tPRWatch   Duration `yaml:\"prwatch\"`\n}\n\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n../../docs/site/configuration.md:30:| `intervals.prwatch` | `5m` | PR watch loop poll (minimum `1m`): checks ard's open pull requests for human merges, closes without merge, long-pending checks and red drafts |\n../../docs/proposal.md:310:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners. Within a class, the priority labels `ard:p0`..`ard:p3` order candidates (unlabelled = P2, several = the highest), then oldest first. Priority never crosses classes: a P3 review still starts before a P0 triage job, and at most a P0 waits for work already finishing. A relabel takes effect on the next poll. Triage and workers also run one extra search per role per tick for `ard:p0`/`ard:p1`, so a P0 beyond the oldest 50 for its role is still found. Reviewers rank by the higher of the PR's and its linked issue's label; they search only PRs, so a P0 label that is only on the issue is ranked but not searched for beyond the oldest 50 (a gap we accept). A grant does not clear the `pool.starved` warning; it re-arms when the class has no wait over the threshold, and the warning names the key and priority of the oldest waiter. In dry-run the `would_claim` lines carry `priority` and come out in rank order. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot would otherwise sit idle until that loop's next poll. To avoid that, a released slot (or a raised `max_pods`) wakes the loops with live demand for one tick, after a short debounce (default 2s) so a burst of releases makes one wake; those loops offer as usual, so class order and reservations still decide, and a paused controller ignores the wake. The merger, prwatch and scanners are never woken this way. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n../../docs/proposal.md:430:A PR can change state behind ard's back, or stop moving, and the merger sees only ready, open PRs. `internal/prwatch` is a controller loop, not an agent, and takes no slot. Every `intervals.prwatch` (default 5m) it reads ard's PRs (App-authored `worker/` branches with base `main`: draft, ready, merged and closed) and **only observes, records and notifies**. It never merges, updates a branch, re-requests a review or starts a round: those belong to the merger, to #252 (stale reviews), #255 (conflicts) and `reviewfix` (#41), and the loop's effects interface has no such call. It is skipped while the controller is paused, like the merger.\n../../docs/proposal.md:440:Checks are judged by `prgate.EvalChecks`, the same function the merger uses, so the two cannot read one set of checks differently. The loop acts only on PRs it has seen open: it keeps a row per PR in the `pr_watch` table of `_controller.db` (head, checks state, when the head was first seen, and what was notified), so installing it does not replay history. Per tick it makes one `ListPRs(state=all, base=main)` call and reads `RequiredChecks` once, and check runs and commit statuses only for open PRs whose head is not settled; a green or red result is kept per head, so an unchanged head costs no check calls (at most 1+2N calls). A PR with a running worker or review job is skipped. Each action runs at most once per (PR, head, transition): the loop checks the row, a `pr_watch` event `{pr, head, transition}` and a marker comment (`<!-- ard:prwatch <transition> pr=<n> head=<sha> -->`) before it acts, and writes the event and row after the effect, so a restart or a failed write posts nothing twice. Check names come from CI and are untrusted: they are sanitized and printed in code spans, and no PR or issue body text or `@` is ever copied. In `--dry-run` the loop keeps its state in memory, appends no events and writes only to the effects ledger. `ard serve --loops` can leave it out.\n../../docs/proposal.md:474:intervals: { scanners: 4h, triage: 15m, workers: 5m, reviewers: 5m, merger: 5m, prwatch: 5m }\n../../docs/proposal.md:475:pr_watch: { pending_timeout: 2h }   # at least intervals.prwatch, at most 7d\n../../docs/layout.md:51:internal/prgate/            decisions shared by the reviewer, the merger and prwatch: reviews-passed-at-head (label rule, head Covers, reviewed heads), ParseWorkerBranch, and the check rule (EvalChecks, ResolveApps)\n../../docs/layout.md:54:internal/prwatch/           the PR watch loop (#267): observes ard's PRs for closed-unmerged, long-pending checks, human merges and red drafts; labels, comments and events only, never merges; no slot","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c9504e90-0a46-4f9b-9664-2e88cac7e920","uuid":"103883d0-4978-4fff-ba8f-436851e7fa01","timestamp":"2026-11-25T13:58:11.694Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\tdefaultIntakeMode      = IntakeLabel\n\n\tdefaultIntervalScanners  = Duration(4 * time.Hour)\n\tdefaultIntervalTriage    = Duration(15 * time.Minute)\n\tdefaultIntervalWorkers   = Duration(5 * time.Minute)\n\tdefaultIntervalReviewers = Duration(5 * time.Minute)\n\tdefaultIntervalMerger    = Duration(5 * time.Minute)\n\tdefaultIntervalPRWatch   = Duration(5 * time.Minute)\n\n\tdefaultPRWatchPendingTimeout = Duration(2 * time.Hour)\n\tmaxPRWatchPendingTimeout     = Duration(7 * 24 * time.Hour)\n\t\tBudgetUSDPerDay: defaultBudgetUSDPerDay,\n\t\tMaxBudgetUSD:    defaultMaxBudgetUSD,\n\t\tGlobalMaxOpen:   defaultGlobalMaxOpen,\n\t\tIntervals: Intervals{\n\t\t\tScanners:  defaultIntervalScanners,\n\t\t\tTriage:    defaultIntervalTriage,\n\t\t\tWorkers:   defaultIntervalWorkers,\n\t\t\tReviewers: defaultIntervalReviewers,\n\t\t\tMerger:    defaultIntervalMerger,\n\t\t\tPRWatch:   defaultIntervalPRWatch,\n\t\t},\n\t\tPRWatch: PRWatch{PendingTimeout: defaultPRWatchPendingTimeout},\n\t\tIntake:  Intake{Mode: defaultIntakeMode},\n\tfor _, iv := range []struct {\n\t\tkey string\n\t\td   Duration\n\t}{\n\t\t{\"intervals.scanners\", res.Intervals.Scanners},\n\t\t{\"intervals.triage\", res.Intervals.Triage},\n\t\t{\"intervals.workers\", res.Intervals.Workers},\n\t\t{\"intervals.reviewers\", res.Intervals.Reviewers},\n\t\t{\"intervals.merger\", res.Intervals.Merger},\n\t\t{\"intervals.prwatch\", res.Intervals.PRWatch},\n\t} {\n\t\tif iv.d < minLoopInterval {\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, &Error{File: cfgPath, Key: iv.key, Msg: fmt.Sprintf(\"must be at least 1m (the Reaper alone runs every minute), got %s\", iv.d)})\n\t\t}\n\t}\nfunc Keys() []string {\n\treturn []string{\n\t\t\"max_pods\", \"executor\", \"agent\", \"require_approval\",\n\t\t\"budget_usd_per_day\", \"tokens_per_day\", \"max_budget_usd\", \"global_max_open\", \"paused\",\n\t\t\"intervals.scanners\", \"intervals.triage\", \"intervals.workers\",\n\t\t\"intervals.reviewers\", \"intervals.merger\", \"intervals.prwatch\",\n\t\t\"pr_watch.pending_timeout\", \"roles_repo\",\n\t\t\"intake.mode\", \"intake.label\",\n\t\t\"merge.protected_paths\",\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc (ri *rawIntervals) parse(n *yaml.Node, file, prefix string) Errors {\n\tvar errs Errors\n\tif n.Tag == \"!!null\" {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tif n.Kind != yaml.MappingNode {\n\t\treturn Errors{{File: file, Key: prefix, Msg: \"must be a mapping of loop intervals\"}}\n\t}\n\tset := func(name string, dst **Duration) {\n\t\tvalue := mappingValue(n, name)\n\t\tif value == nil {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t\tvar d Duration\n\t\tbefore := len(errs)\n\t\terrs = append(errs, decodeValue(value, file, prefix+\".\"+name, &d)...)\n\t\tif len(errs) == before {\n\t\t\t*dst = &d\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tset(\"scanners\", &ri.scanners)\n\tset(\"triage\", &ri.triage)\n\tset(\"workers\", &ri.workers)\n\tset(\"reviewers\", &ri.reviewers)\n\tset(\"merger\", &ri.merger)\n\tset(\"prwatch\", &ri.prwatch)\n\tfor i := 0; i < len(n.Content); i += 2 {\n\t\tname := n.Content[i].Value\n\t\tswitch name {\n\t\tcase \"scanners\", \"triage\", \"workers\", \"reviewers\", \"merger\", \"prwatch\":\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, &Error{File: file, Key: prefix + \".\" + name, Msg: \"unknown key (want one of scanners, triage, workers, reviewers, merger, prwatch)\"})\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn errs\n}\n\nfunc (raw *rawConfig) parseMerge(n *yaml.Node, file string) Errors {\ntype intervalsYAML struct {\n\tScanners  Duration `yaml:\"scanners\"`\n\tTriage    Duration `yaml:\"triage\"`\n\tWorkers   Duration `yaml:\"workers\"`\n\tReviewers Duration `yaml:\"reviewers\"`\n\tMerger    Duration `yaml:\"merger\"`\n\tPRWatch   Duration `yaml:\"prwatch\"`\n}\n\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n../../docs/site/configuration.md:30:| `intervals.prwatch` | `5m` | PR watch loop poll (minimum `1m`): checks ard's open pull requests for human merges, closes without merge, long-pending checks and red drafts |\n../../docs/proposal.md:310:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners. Within a class, the priority labels `ard:p0`..`ard:p3` order candidates (unlabelled = P2, several = the highest), then oldest first. Priority never crosses classes: a P3 review still starts before a P0 triage job, and at most a P0 waits for work already finishing. A relabel takes effect on the next poll. Triage and workers also run one extra search per role per tick for `ard:p0`/`ard:p1`, so a P0 beyond the oldest 50 for its role is still found. Reviewers rank by the higher of the PR's and its linked issue's label; they search only PRs, so a P0 label that is only on the issue is ranked but not searched for beyond the oldest 50 (a gap we accept). A grant does not clear the `pool.starved` warning; it re-arms when the class has no wait over the threshold, and the warning names the key and priority of the oldest waiter. In dry-run the `would_claim` lines carry `priority` and come out in rank order. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot would otherwise sit idle until that loop's next poll. To avoid that, a released slot (or a raised `max_pods`) wakes the loops with live demand for one tick, after a short debounce (default 2s) so a burst of releases makes one wake; those loops offer as usual, so class order and reservations still decide, and a paused controller ignores the wake. The merger, prwatch and scanners are never woken this way. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n../../docs/proposal.md:430:A PR can change state behind ard's back, or stop moving, and the merger sees only ready, open PRs. `internal/prwatch` is a controller loop, not an agent, and takes no slot. Every `intervals.prwatch` (default 5m) it reads ard's PRs (App-authored `worker/` branches with base `main`: draft, ready, merged and closed) and **only observes, records and notifies**. It never merges, updates a branch, re-requests a review or starts a round: those belong to the merger, to #252 (stale reviews), #255 (conflicts) and `reviewfix` (#41), and the loop's effects interface has no such call. It is skipped while the controller is paused, like the merger.\n../../docs/proposal.md:440:Checks are judged by `prgate.EvalChecks`, the same function the merger uses, so the two cannot read one set of checks differently. The loop acts only on PRs it has seen open: it keeps a row per PR in the `pr_watch` table of `_controller.db` (head, checks state, when the head was first seen, and what was notified), so installing it does not replay history. Per tick it makes one `ListPRs(state=all, base=main)` call and reads `RequiredChecks` once, and check runs and commit statuses only for open PRs whose head is not settled; a green or red result is kept per head, so an unchanged head costs no check calls (at most 1+2N calls). A PR with a running worker or review job is skipped. Each action runs at most once per (PR, head, transition): the loop checks the row, a `pr_watch` event `{pr, head, transition}` and a marker comment (`<!-- ard:prwatch <transition> pr=<n> head=<sha> -->`) before it acts, and writes the event and row after the effect, so a restart or a failed write posts nothing twice. Check names come from CI and are untrusted: they are sanitized and printed in code spans, and no PR or issue body text or `@` is ever copied. In `--dry-run` the loop keeps its state in memory, appends no events and writes only to the effects ledger. `ard serve --loops` can leave it out.\n../../docs/proposal.md:474:intervals: { scanners: 4h, triage: 15m, workers: 5m, reviewers: 5m, merger: 5m, prwatch: 5m }\n../../docs/proposal.md:475:pr_watch: { pending_timeout: 2h }   # at least intervals.prwatch, at most 7d\n../../docs/layout.md:51:internal/prgate/            decisions shared by the reviewer, the merger and prwatch: reviews-passed-at-head (label rule, head Covers, reviewed heads), ParseWorkerBranch, and the check rule (EvalChecks, ResolveApps)\n../../docs/layout.md:54:internal/prwatch/           the PR watch loop (#267): observes ard's PRs for closed-unmerged, long-pending checks, human merges and red drafts; labels, comments and events only, never merges; no slot","st