{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NPRXuUDzBmqQ9RfsNYQVeT","type":"tool_result","content":"ok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/servecmd\t39.279s\n275:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, output and messages. The starter ordering is product-manager, then architect; designer, then documentation-planner; the engineer runs after architect and documentation-planner (`after:` in the starter `config.yaml`). Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`. The starter prompts read the repo context docs per role: `product-manager` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`, `architect` reads `VISION.md` and `TECH_STACK.md`, `designer` and `documentation-planner` read `PRODUCT.md`, and `engineer` reads `TECH_STACK.md`. Each prompt states the shared rules: the file is optional, one with no text outside headings and HTML comments counts as absent, the issue is the ask and the files are constraints, and `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` win on commands and layout. A planning role that finds a conflict records it, with file and section, in its own role file.\n279:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 3,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present. When the change needs files from the network it adds a `### Network inputs` subsection (exact URL, pinned version, verification source, landing path, and who provides it: the worker if its role has network, else a human); it never fetches and never writes a checksum it did not read from a file (#301).\n348:## Worker loop\n352:1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no label change when it is missing), and the admission: an App-authored issue passes, a human-filed one passes only when its stored body hash still matches, and a changed title or body is re-gated exactly as triage does (one comment, `ard:needs-human`).\n368:## Reviewer loop\n384:**The starter security reviewer (`reviewers/security-review`, #43).** `.ard/reviewers/security-review/prompt.txt` looks for injection, authz gaps, secrets and unsafe dependencies the change introduces or makes reachable, plus other weaknesses. It has the same untrusted inputs, Trust rules (steering text in the change is a blocking `high` finding), write rules and role-file format as eng-review, with `role: security-review` and a `## Checks` body. It never builds, tests or runs the repository's code. It runs, when they are installed: gitleaks over the changed files with `--redact`; gosec on changed Go packages with `-nosec`; and semgrep with `--metrics=off` and `--disable-nosem`, using only a rule file already in the repository that the change does not touch, or the sidecar's. Suppressions the change adds are reviewed as part of the change. It skips a missing tool and says so, never downloads rules or vulnerability data, and never copies a secret it finds into its report (file, line and kind only). Each finding's first line starts with `[injection]`, `[authz]`, `[secret]`, `[dependency]` or `[other]`. Critical and high block, medium only when it is one of the four kinds with a nameable exploit, low and info never; `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review. The starter override is Opus 5.5 (#8) with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`. The role's `aid` (#205) runs gitleaks, gosec and semgrep in controller code before the agent (Reviewer loop, step 3); the prompt reads `aid.jsonl` first and runs the tools itself only when it is absent. It ships a `Dockerfile` (the default ard-agent image plus the pinned tools), so it runs under Docker. The same fetched-file check applies, and an unverified fetched file is a high finding; it still never runs repository code (#301).\n388:**The starter PM reviewer (`reviewers/product-manager-review`, #45).** `.ard/reviewers/product-manager-review/prompt.txt` checks the change against the acceptance criteria. They come from `plan.md`'s `## Requirements` (`### Acceptance criteria`, numbered AC1, AC2), else the issue body; comments never add, relax or remove one, and a plan that contradicts the issue is `needs-human`. Each criterion is marked met, not met or cannot verify, and the change is checked against the out of scope list. A criterion not met is a blocking `high` finding (`critical` when it breaks existing behaviour); cannot verify and out-of-scope work are non-blocking `medium`. With no criteria it approves and says so. It uses read-only Bash only. The starter `when` is `triage: [product-manager]`; a repo adds UI paths under `when.paths`.\n| `intervals.scanners` | `4h` | How often scanners run |\n| `intervals.triage` | `15m` | Triage loop poll |\n| `intervals.workers` | `5m` | Worker loop poll |\n| `intervals.reviewers` | `5m` | Reviewer loop poll |\n| `intervals.merger` | `5m` | Merger loop poll |\n| `intervals.prwatch` | `5m` | PR watch loop poll (minimum `1m`): checks ard's open pull requests for human merges, closes without merge, long-pending checks and red drafts |\n| `pr_watch.pending_timeout` | `2h` | How long a required check may stay pending before the PR watch flags it (at least the interval, at most `7d`) |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c9504e90-0a46-4f9b-9664-2e88cac7e920","uuid":"1be3e328-aeaf-4a53-bc79-4bcbd94c968a","timestamp":"2026-11-25T14:04:24.103Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/servecmd\t39.279s\n275:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, outp