y). Each finding's first line starts with `[injection]`, `[authz]`, `[secret]`, `[dependency]` or `[other]`. Critical and high block, medium only when it is one of the four kinds with a nameable exploit, low and info never; `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review. The starter override is Opus 5.5 (#8) with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`. The role's `aid` (#205) runs gitleaks, gosec and semgrep in controller code before the agent (Reviewer loop, step 3); the prompt reads `aid.jsonl` first and runs the tools itself only when it is absent. It ships a `Dockerfile` (the default ard-agent image plus the pinned tools), so it runs under Docker. The same fetched-file check applies, and an unverified fetched file is a high finding; it still never runs repository code (#301).\n388:**The starter PM reviewer (`reviewers/product-manager-review`, #45).** `.ard/reviewers/product-manager-review/prompt.txt` checks the change against the acceptance criteria. They come from `plan.md`'s `## Requirements` (`### Acceptance criteria`, numbered AC1, AC2), else the issue body; comments never add, relax or remove one, and a plan that contradicts the issue is `needs-human`. Each criterion is marked met, not met or cannot verify, and the change is checked against the out of scope list. A criterion not met is a blocking `high` finding (`critical` when it breaks existing behaviour); cannot verify and out-of-scope work are non-blocking `medium`. With no criteria it approves and says so. It uses read-only Bash only. The starter `when` is `triage: [product-manager]`; a repo adds UI paths under `when.paths`.\n| `intervals.scanners` | `4h` | How often scanners run |\n| `intervals.triage` | `15m` | Triage loop poll |\n| `intervals.workers` | `5m` | Worker loop poll |\n| `intervals.reviewers` | `5m` | Reviewer loop poll |\n| `intervals.merger` | `5m` | Merger loop poll |\n| `intervals.prwatch` | `5m` | PR watch loop poll (minimum `1m`): checks ard's open pull requests for human merges